-- Opaque types: `DECLARE T` with no body.
--
-- Run this file with:  l4 run doc/reference/types/opaque-example.l4

§ `Naming a type without describing it`

-- A licensing rule cares that an applicant and a premises are DIFFERENT KINDS
-- of thing, and that a decision about one is not accidentally applied to the
-- other. It does not care what either is made of. An opaque type says exactly
-- that much and no more.
DECLARE Applicant
DECLARE Premises

-- These type-check. `Applicant` and `Premises` are distinct types, so swapping
-- the two arguments below would be a type error.
GIVEN a IS AN Applicant
      p IS A Premises
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may be licensed` a p IS TRUE

§ `Opaque types inside records`

DECLARE Outcome IS ONE OF granted, refused

DECLARE Application
  HAS applicant IS AN Applicant
      premises  IS A Premises
      outcome   IS AN Outcome

-- A rule that never looks inside the opaque fields evaluates normally.
GIVEN app IS AN Application
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `was granted` app IF app's outcome EQUALS granted

§ `Where the values come from`

-- No expression in this file can produce an `Applicant`: an opaque type has no
-- constructors. A value arrives from outside — an ASSUME here, a JSON input at
-- a service boundary in production.
ASSUME `the applicant` IS AN Applicant
ASSUME `the premises` IS A Premises

#EVAL `was granted` (Application WITH applicant IS `the applicant`,
                                 premises  IS `the premises`,
                                 outcome   IS granted)

#EVAL `was granted` (Application WITH applicant IS `the applicant`,
                                 premises  IS `the premises`,
                                 outcome   IS refused)

#CHECK `may be licensed`

§ `Opaque types with parameters`

-- The head's arguments are type variables, as they are for a record head.
DECLARE Bag x

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
DECLARE Register a

GIVEN b IS A Bag OF NUMBER
      r IS A Register OF STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `both present` b r IS TRUE

#CHECK `both present`
